WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.572 --> 00:00:13.021
I would like to introduce
today's speakers.

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:18.670
Larry Lagerstrom, who you'll be
hearing from later in this session,

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:21.990
is the Acting Director of
Academic Programs responsible for

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:24.575
graduate education offerings.

00:00:24.575 --> 00:00:27.750
Today's featured presenters
are Bruce Wallace and

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:30.670
Reetika Grewal from Silicon Valley Bank.

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:36.130
Bruce Wallace is the Chief Digital Officer
of Silicon Valley Bank Financial Group.

00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:39.990
He is responsible for
client digital banking experience and

00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:44.300
channel delivery services along
with the sales, development, and

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:48.610
delivery for fee-based products
including payments, cash management,

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:54.440
cards, merchant services, foreign
exchange, and global treasury services.

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:58.300
Previously, Bruce was Silicon Valley
Bank's Chief Operations Officer,

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:00.910
responsible for global operations and IT.

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:05.920
Prior to joining Silicon Valley Bank,
Bruce spent more than 20 years in

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:09.620
a variety of management positions
with Wells Fargo and Company.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:14.640
Reetika Grewal is the Head of Payment,
Strategy, and

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:17.200
Solutions at Silicon Valley Bank.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:22.479
She joined the company in 2012 to lead
the Payment, Strategy, and Solutions team.

00:01:22.479 --> 00:01:26.434
This team focuses on internal
payment strategy and development,

00:01:26.434 --> 00:01:30.743
as well as working collaboratively
with Silicon Valley Bank clients and

00:01:30.743 --> 00:01:34.998
partners to help deliver payment
solutions to the market.

00:01:34.998 --> 00:01:38.148
She leads Silicon Valley Bank's
partnership with MasterCard to run

00:01:38.148 --> 00:01:42.750
Commerce.innovated, an accelerator
program focused on helping early stage

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:47.030
companies innovating
across the commerce space.

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:50.500
Prior to Silicon Valley Bank,
Reetika was at JPMorgan Chase.

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:59.980
Reetika was recently awarded as one of
the most influential women in payments.

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:03.820
Actually, she won this honor for
the last two years.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:06.820
At this time, I'd like to turn
the floor over to Bruce and Reetika.

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:10.430
&gt;&gt; Good morning, everyone, this is Bruce.

00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:15.920
And we are excited to be here this morning
to talk about what's happening with

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:17.260
the Fintech revolution.

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:21.532
So, we're going to have some slides that
we're going to walk through as Beth had

00:02:21.532 --> 00:02:22.274
mentioned.

00:02:22.274 --> 00:02:25.155
We'll be more than happy to
answer questions throughout

00:02:25.155 --> 00:02:27.016
the course of the presentation, and

00:02:27.016 --> 00:02:30.989
actually would recommend that you
provide questions throughout the course.

00:02:30.989 --> 00:02:32.884
So I'm going to turn it over to Reetika,

00:02:32.884 --> 00:02:35.732
who's going to start stepping
through the presentation.

00:02:35.732 --> 00:02:36.277
Reetika?

00:02:36.277 --> 00:02:38.628
&gt;&gt; Great, good morning everyone.

00:02:38.628 --> 00:02:43.397
So I'm going to start first with
a slide that we've received

00:02:43.397 --> 00:02:47.988
-- some data from CB Insights,
which is an analysts firm.

00:02:47.988 --> 00:02:52.270
-- and what is going on in this
slide is really to help illustrate

00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:54.710
the Fintech innovation in here.

00:02:54.710 --> 00:03:00.140
What they did was use the Wells Fargo
homepage as an example of the top half

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:05.620
is the consumer home page, and the bottom
half is the small business home page,

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:12.145
and really describe all of the different
companies that are innovating and

00:03:12.145 --> 00:03:17.295
attacking every single segment
of banking from lending,

00:03:17.295 --> 00:03:20.675
to banking services themselves,
to investing.

00:03:20.675 --> 00:03:24.822
There are a slew of companies
that are going after and

00:03:24.822 --> 00:03:28.600
truly disrupting banking
as you know it today.

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:33.713
On the consumer side, there's some
names that I'm sure you've heard

00:03:33.713 --> 00:03:39.522
of in the market, right, on the lending
side, companies like SoFi and others.

00:03:39.522 --> 00:03:44.448
As well as on the banking side,
some of the banking startups that

00:03:44.448 --> 00:03:49.560
are trying to disrupt the way that
consumers receive just the basic

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:54.960
services that come along with a bank
account, companies like Moven.

00:03:56.510 --> 00:04:01.848
There's a slew of companies focused
on the savings side: savings,

00:04:01.848 --> 00:04:08.027
wealth management, companies like Digit
that help to automate your savings.

00:04:08.027 --> 00:04:12.050
Companies like Betterment,
Wealthfront, etc.,

00:04:12.050 --> 00:04:16.080
that are focused on that
wealth management space.

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:20.500
And then finally there's a whole world
of companies that are focused on sort

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:25.305
of the payments aspect from
a consumer perspective as well.

00:04:25.305 --> 00:04:30.072
Companies like Bill.com that
help make bill payments easier,

00:04:30.072 --> 00:04:34.660
cleaner, and
with a beautiful UI in front of it.

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:39.440
On the small business side
there's even more pronounced

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:44.220
disruption that's happening from,
again, same thing,

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:47.805
where pretty much every
tab of that display

00:04:47.805 --> 00:04:52.404
from the Wells Fargo example
here is being disrupted.

00:04:52.404 --> 00:04:56.231
You can look at, again,
banking, lending and credit,

00:04:56.231 --> 00:05:00.603
and I'm sorry, merchant services,
insurance, all of those

00:05:00.603 --> 00:05:04.998
things are all being disrupted again
by a wide variety of services.

00:05:04.998 --> 00:05:08.673
Some of the more well-known
services because they've IPOed or

00:05:08.673 --> 00:05:13.027
are out in the market really aggressively,
companies like Square that have

00:05:13.027 --> 00:05:16.786
truly disrupted the way merchant
services are being delivered.

00:05:16.786 --> 00:05:18.450
Giving small businesses,

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:23.368
extending the aperture of small businesses
that are able to accept card payments

00:05:23.368 --> 00:05:26.853
as well as changing the way
people accept card payments.

00:05:26.853 --> 00:05:31.995
And then on the lending side
companies like Kabbage and

00:05:31.995 --> 00:05:38.716
OnDeck helping small businesses get
access to capital in unique ways.

00:05:38.716 --> 00:05:40.424
Is there anything else that
you would add on this slide?

00:05:40.424 --> 00:05:44.597
&gt;&gt; Just one thing I would add at the macro
level is, I think what's happened

00:05:44.597 --> 00:05:49.387
over the past, I would say, three to five
years, with a lot of these companies is,

00:05:49.387 --> 00:05:52.487
every single one on there
is a pure digital solution.

00:05:52.487 --> 00:05:57.649
So I think what's happening is,
there's a lot of disruption specifically

00:05:57.649 --> 00:06:03.378
around companies coming into the financial
services space without the traditional

00:06:03.378 --> 00:06:08.800
infrastructure required, with a purely
digital or mobile based experience.

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:12.632
&gt;&gt; Okay, yeah, and we're going to talk
a little bit about some of the main

00:06:12.632 --> 00:06:17.407
disruption, or some of the main drivers
that's causing this inflow of disruption.

00:06:17.407 --> 00:06:21.459
And we will talk a little bit more
about how digital comes into play here.

00:06:21.459 --> 00:06:26.134
So the next slide is to sort of see
the parallel that's happening in the UK.

00:06:26.134 --> 00:06:31.212
It's a very similar trend where again,
consumer banking,

00:06:31.212 --> 00:06:36.701
business banking is being disrupted
by a whole slew of startups.

00:06:36.701 --> 00:06:41.997
Obviously the Brexit news is
fresh in everyone's mind,

00:06:41.997 --> 00:06:46.644
so it'll be interesting
to see how this evolves.

00:06:46.644 --> 00:06:49.478
But given that SVB has
an office in London,

00:06:49.478 --> 00:06:53.436
we get a good front row seat into
seeing a lot of this as well.

00:06:53.436 --> 00:06:54.866
And it's, again,

00:06:54.866 --> 00:07:00.258
very similar in nature to the destruction
that's happening on the US side.

00:07:00.258 --> 00:07:06.257
And attacking pretty much every
aspect of banking, lending,

00:07:06.257 --> 00:07:12.610
investing, etc, that consumers and
businesses are focused on.

00:07:12.610 --> 00:07:16.117
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so there were a couple questions
that I think were pretty interesting that

00:07:16.117 --> 00:07:17.885
came up related to the first two slides.

00:07:17.885 --> 00:07:21.385
The first one was, where is Blockchain?

00:07:21.385 --> 00:07:25.824
So in terms of what's happening
with the percentage of startups

00:07:25.824 --> 00:07:29.359
that are in Blockchain or
digital currencies, and

00:07:29.359 --> 00:07:34.211
in terms of overall venture investing,
there's been approximately

00:07:34.211 --> 00:07:38.424
a billion dollars that have
gone into Blockchain startups.

00:07:38.424 --> 00:07:43.076
And in terms of where it stacks against
all of the other startups that Reetika had

00:07:43.076 --> 00:07:47.660
been referencing earlier, it's probably
about the sixth or seventh largest

00:07:47.660 --> 00:07:52.127
sector in terms of where a lot of the
investment dollars are going right now.

00:07:52.127 --> 00:07:55.355
It' s primarily going
into the lending space.

00:07:55.355 --> 00:07:59.849
It's going into the payment space.

00:07:59.849 --> 00:08:04.681
And it's also going into kind of
a combination of individual finance,

00:08:04.681 --> 00:08:10.238
consumer finance, and then also into
different types of individual investing,

00:08:10.238 --> 00:08:15.340
in consumer investing, or
I'm sorry, business investing.

00:08:15.340 --> 00:08:19.421
But if you look at the trajectory
around investments

00:08:19.421 --> 00:08:24.165
in blockchain technology,
it is on a very high trajectory.

00:08:24.165 --> 00:08:28.937
And in terms of what's happened so
far in the first half of this year,

00:08:28.937 --> 00:08:34.470
there has been no tampering of investments
going on in Bitcoin and Blockchain.

00:08:34.470 --> 00:08:38.463
And the second question was related
to emerging markets such as Africa,

00:08:38.463 --> 00:08:39.778
Latin America, Asia.

00:08:39.778 --> 00:08:43.437
I would say, again,
in terms of investment dollars,

00:08:43.437 --> 00:08:48.528
probably about 75 to 80% of the overall
venture investment dollars for

00:08:48.528 --> 00:08:52.680
Fintech are primarily going in
North America in the UK and EU.

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:57.594
I think in terms of some of the emerging
countries because they still have a very

00:08:57.594 --> 00:09:02.807
high unbanked or underbanked population,
a lot of these startups that are in those

00:09:02.807 --> 00:09:07.940
specific spaces are really tackling
much more simplified financial services.

00:09:07.940 --> 00:09:11.270
I'm just trying to get basic
consumer products such as

00:09:11.270 --> 00:09:16.060
mobile payments into the hands of
million of consumers that are unbanked.

00:09:16.060 --> 00:09:18.960
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and the other thing I'll say
about that is in reference to some of

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:24.380
the other markets is the US and
the UK are very similar in terms

00:09:24.380 --> 00:09:29.160
of people leveraging credit cards and
merchant services and things like that.

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:32.658
And in those markets, that basic
infrastructure isn't even there.

00:09:32.658 --> 00:09:36.237
Right, so if you go to Brazil,
for example,

00:09:36.237 --> 00:09:41.623
the maturation of credit cards is
nowhere near as deep as it is here.

00:09:41.623 --> 00:09:44.912
So it's similar to what you were saying in
terms of leveraging alternative ways of

00:09:44.912 --> 00:09:45.540
moving money.

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:50.790
&gt;&gt; While Reetika goes to the next slide,
we're getting a lot of great questions.

00:09:50.790 --> 00:09:54.280
I'm doing my best to kind of synthesize
them together because there's some common

00:09:54.280 --> 00:09:56.980
questions, so just to let everyone know.

00:09:56.980 --> 00:09:57.718
And a few of them,

00:09:57.718 --> 00:10:00.192
we're going to specifically
address on some future slides.

00:10:00.192 --> 00:10:03.555
So I am watching everyone's questions,
just wanted to let you know that.

00:10:03.555 --> 00:10:07.549
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and the next slide really is

00:10:07.549 --> 00:10:12.787
meant to focus on why is the financial
services under attack so

00:10:12.787 --> 00:10:16.024
aggressively by all these startups?

00:10:16.024 --> 00:10:20.467
And I think it's because
financial services represent

00:10:20.467 --> 00:10:23.590
20% of the S&amp;P 500 Index.

00:10:23.590 --> 00:10:28.010
And if you just look at the names of the
companies that are in the middle of this

00:10:28.010 --> 00:10:33.350
target, they are household names
that have been in the business

00:10:33.350 --> 00:10:38.120
of banking and providing payment for
years and years,

00:10:38.120 --> 00:10:44.922
hundreds of years in many cases.

00:10:44.922 --> 00:10:51.384
And I have a colleague who's from Texas,
and he always talks about Texas tea.

00:10:51.384 --> 00:10:57.514
And the banking global financial
institution market is in the trillions,

00:10:57.514 --> 00:11:04.241
8.5 trillion, so again, reiterating
the prize at the end of the disruption.

00:11:04.241 --> 00:11:09.090
And this is sort of an interesting fact
from Accenture that they are estimating

00:11:09.090 --> 00:11:14.157
that full service banks, meaning the banks
that offer sort of consumer banking and

00:11:14.157 --> 00:11:18.859
payments and things like that similar
to like a Bank of America, JP Morgan,

00:11:18.859 --> 00:11:24.147
Wells Fargo, etc, could lose 35% of their
market share globally to digitally and

00:11:24.147 --> 00:11:25.632
oriented disrupters.

00:11:25.632 --> 00:11:28.193
And sort of to Bruce's point earlier,

00:11:28.193 --> 00:11:32.940
it is digitally that these guys
are bringing their solutions to market.

00:11:34.470 --> 00:11:39.074
&gt;&gt; I would say the two other factors
that are primarily involved that we see,

00:11:39.074 --> 00:11:42.785
with respect to a lot of
the Fintech disruption, is one is,

00:11:42.785 --> 00:11:47.039
I think a lot of these solutions
are offering speed and simplicity.

00:11:47.039 --> 00:11:51.666
And I think that almost everybody on the
call probably has a personal experience of

00:11:51.666 --> 00:11:56.024
dealing with a traditional financial
institution that in terms of how long it

00:11:56.024 --> 00:12:00.315
took to finally get a product delivered or
to get your mortgage refinanced or

00:12:00.315 --> 00:12:01.690
whatever it is.

00:12:01.690 --> 00:12:05.568
Many times it's kind of a long
cumbersome process to go through that.

00:12:05.568 --> 00:12:09.307
And so a lot of the business models
that we see that are being successful in

00:12:09.307 --> 00:12:11.883
getting traction,
they really focus on speed and

00:12:11.883 --> 00:12:15.130
simplicity in terms of
delivering the product.

00:12:15.130 --> 00:12:17.670
And the second one, which Reetika
referenced a little bit earlier,

00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:21.400
but I'd just like to highlight
a little bit more, and

00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:24.400
that is,
it's really around the user experience.

00:12:24.400 --> 00:12:29.064
I think that a lot of the Fintech
disruptors are very focused on starting

00:12:29.064 --> 00:12:33.588
with an experience that the consumer
of the business expects to get.

00:12:33.588 --> 00:12:37.765
That's maybe a little bit more aligned
with what they would get from another

00:12:37.765 --> 00:12:42.071
software company or another technology
company, as opposed to trying to kind

00:12:42.071 --> 00:12:45.610
of just provide a better
financial services product.

00:12:45.610 --> 00:12:49.959
Many times it's not focused on the product
because, as I think everyone would agree,

00:12:49.959 --> 00:12:53.548
many financial services products
are very commodity based products.

00:12:53.548 --> 00:12:58.590
There's not a lot of differentiation
around a checking account or a debit card.

00:12:58.590 --> 00:13:02.155
However, there can be massive
differentiation in the user experience in

00:13:02.155 --> 00:13:03.196
using the products.

00:13:03.196 --> 00:13:08.064
And so, speed, simplicity in the user
experience are kind of the real key areas

00:13:08.064 --> 00:13:12.550
that the innovators are focused
on in trying to get market share.

00:13:12.550 --> 00:13:17.258
&gt;&gt; Yeah and that's a great transition to
sort of the macro level themes that we see

00:13:17.258 --> 00:13:20.387
really the trends shaping
the Fintech landscape.

00:13:20.387 --> 00:13:25.054
And so we talked a little bit about
some of the technology that's fueling

00:13:25.054 --> 00:13:26.755
the disruption, right.

00:13:26.755 --> 00:13:31.569
The barriers to entry for entering in and
bringing a startup to market

00:13:31.569 --> 00:13:36.060
are significantly lower than they
have been in previous years.

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:40.398
Thanks to wonderful things
like the mobile devices and

00:13:40.398 --> 00:13:45.491
the iTunes marketplace, I'm sorry,
the app store, where you

00:13:45.491 --> 00:13:51.450
can just get your product in front of
millions of eyeballs pretty quickly.

00:13:52.490 --> 00:13:56.600
As well as things like being able to
host your service in the Cloud without

00:13:56.600 --> 00:13:59.700
having to invest a whole bunch of money in

00:13:59.700 --> 00:14:01.980
infrastructure to bring
your solution to market.

00:14:01.980 --> 00:14:08.700
And then lastly, leveraging the networks,
the social networks and

00:14:10.190 --> 00:14:15.690
the other kinds of networks to
distribute your solution to market.

00:14:15.690 --> 00:14:20.697
So leveraging services like Twitter and
Facebook and Medium and

00:14:20.697 --> 00:14:25.982
all those other channels to really
distribute marketing at a very,

00:14:25.982 --> 00:14:31.282
very low cost have made the barriers
to entry significantly lower.

00:14:31.282 --> 00:14:34.590
And it really helps to
fuel the innovation.

00:14:36.930 --> 00:14:40.773
There's also a big trend
around trying to remove paper.

00:14:40.773 --> 00:14:45.560
And this is sort of along the lines
of removing friction from processes,

00:14:45.560 --> 00:14:48.239
and shifting from paper to electronic.

00:14:48.239 --> 00:14:52.017
The idea that checks will
leave our ecosystem is

00:14:52.017 --> 00:14:55.612
a reality that I really
hope happens one day.

00:14:55.612 --> 00:14:56.881
But in the interim,

00:14:56.881 --> 00:15:02.460
the technology around remote deposit
capture has made accepting checks easier.

00:15:02.460 --> 00:15:10.660
And people moving to a more cashless
to a card filled society is really,

00:15:10.660 --> 00:15:13.646
is happening significantly
across business and consumer.

00:15:13.646 --> 00:15:18.079
Businesses like electronic
payments because of the richness

00:15:18.079 --> 00:15:22.775
of data that goes along with
transactions and reconciling data.

00:15:22.775 --> 00:15:27.779
And removing humans from being in the
position to constantly move paper through

00:15:27.779 --> 00:15:32.720
processes is something that businesses
have been looking for for a long time.

00:15:32.720 --> 00:15:37.090
So this shift from paper to
electronic is pretty significant.

00:15:37.090 --> 00:15:40.029
&gt;&gt; So one other comment I would make,
I'll try to synthesize four or

00:15:40.029 --> 00:15:41.692
five questions with a common theme.

00:15:41.692 --> 00:15:46.492
And I think the common theme is
around people understanding the value

00:15:46.492 --> 00:15:50.795
proposition around ease of use and
simplicity and speed, but

00:15:50.795 --> 00:15:53.773
talking a little bit
about How this works in

00:15:53.773 --> 00:15:58.509
terms of the current products that
you use on a day-to-day basis.

00:15:58.509 --> 00:16:00.405
And I think on a day-to-day basis,

00:16:00.405 --> 00:16:03.165
what we're seeing with a lot
of Fintech disruptors.

00:16:03.165 --> 00:16:08.025
Again, that's a common trend is,
they're embedding the payment experience

00:16:08.025 --> 00:16:11.850
directly into how you do
commerce on a day-to-day basis.

00:16:11.850 --> 00:16:16.600
And, I know Uber is a very overused
example in the innovation economy.

00:16:16.600 --> 00:16:21.359
But, they are kind of a perfect example
how they have embedded a financial

00:16:21.359 --> 00:16:26.754
product, which the payment experience
directly into how they use their product.

00:16:26.754 --> 00:16:30.434
And there are many other examples with
Amazon and Apple, and many others.

00:16:30.434 --> 00:16:34.366
Where again, I think that what we
see happening with a lot of this,

00:16:34.366 --> 00:16:39.230
the disrupters is they're actually pushing
payments more into the background.

00:16:39.230 --> 00:16:41.530
It's less around making the payment.

00:16:41.530 --> 00:16:44.530
It's more around making
the payment digitalized and

00:16:44.530 --> 00:16:46.690
embedded in with the commerce experience.

00:16:46.690 --> 00:16:50.720
So the payment is much more of
a utility as opposed to a produce.

00:16:50.720 --> 00:16:52.764
And really again, going back to
what we talked about earlier.

00:16:52.764 --> 00:16:55.202
It's about the user experience, and

00:16:55.202 --> 00:16:58.780
many of the models that
are doing a great job of, again,

00:16:58.780 --> 00:17:04.290
elegantly embedding the financial product
into the experience that you're doing.

00:17:04.290 --> 00:17:07.241
So you don't actually even have to
think about making the payment or

00:17:07.241 --> 00:17:08.909
executing on the financial product.

00:17:08.909 --> 00:17:13.936
Like another example is,
in the small business lending.

00:17:13.936 --> 00:17:18.524
What we have seen is some Fintech
disruptors that are doing a great job of,

00:17:18.524 --> 00:17:21.270
you actually never apply for a loan.

00:17:21.270 --> 00:17:23.210
They just look at everything
in your accounting system.

00:17:23.210 --> 00:17:24.987
They look at your accounts receivable.

00:17:24.987 --> 00:17:28.350
They go ahead and qualify which
ones they will advance credit on.

00:17:28.350 --> 00:17:32.250
And then, it's literally as simple
as pressing one button to say.

00:17:32.250 --> 00:17:34.691
Yes, I would like to get
an advance on my receivables.

00:17:34.691 --> 00:17:39.148
So again, what they're doing is taken
the financial product itself and taken so

00:17:39.148 --> 00:17:42.829
much of the friction out of what you
do to kind of apply and qualify.

00:17:42.829 --> 00:17:46.989
And use it and embed it in to kind of
your day-to-day working experience, or

00:17:46.989 --> 00:17:48.404
your social experience.

00:17:48.404 --> 00:17:53.304
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and for the last big
trend that's really helping to

00:17:53.304 --> 00:17:58.514
shape the Fintech landscape is
the regulatory environment.

00:17:58.514 --> 00:18:03.319
So, one thing that we've been talking
about is when the great recession

00:18:03.319 --> 00:18:04.120
happened.

00:18:04.120 --> 00:18:08.140
Banks sort of took a step back and

00:18:08.140 --> 00:18:13.210
started to divert resources away
from driving innovation, and

00:18:13.210 --> 00:18:18.250
driving new products and services,
and focus more on compliance.

00:18:18.250 --> 00:18:20.880
Making sure that they, safety and

00:18:20.880 --> 00:18:24.605
soundness was sort of
the big mantra of the time.

00:18:24.605 --> 00:18:28.512
And, it always is in the world
of banks [LAUGH] but

00:18:28.512 --> 00:18:33.115
that left open a big gap in
terms of innovation, right?

00:18:33.115 --> 00:18:37.685
So it was a big gap that allowed for
all of the disruptors to come in is

00:18:37.685 --> 00:18:41.777
that when banks stopped thinking
about how do we innovate.

00:18:41.777 --> 00:18:45.190
And Bruce will say that banks are not
really good at innovating in general.

00:18:45.190 --> 00:18:48.424
But when they completely
stopped thinking about that,

00:18:48.424 --> 00:18:51.948
that's really when the whole
world of innovation came out.

00:18:51.948 --> 00:18:55.663
Just the general dissatisfaction with
banks that came out of that, that crisis.

00:18:55.663 --> 00:19:01.212
As well as the focus on
regulatory compliance, and

00:19:01.212 --> 00:19:07.543
the world of regulation is not
easy by any chance, right?

00:19:07.543 --> 00:19:12.061
So, the emergence of the CFPB
happened that during that time where

00:19:12.061 --> 00:19:16.900
there's a very strong champion out
there for consumer protection.

00:19:16.900 --> 00:19:19.596
And then there's,
in the world of payments.

00:19:19.596 --> 00:19:23.159
So you think about things like money
transmitter rules and regulations.

00:19:23.159 --> 00:19:26.954
It's a very complex ecosystem, and

00:19:26.954 --> 00:19:32.464
the financial institutions
are sort of the gate in and

00:19:32.464 --> 00:19:35.536
out of the payments network.

00:19:35.536 --> 00:19:41.067
So, the disruption that's happening, and
the successful disruption that's happening

00:19:41.067 --> 00:19:46.093
is in many ways in partnership with banks
helping to innovate around the front-end

00:19:46.093 --> 00:19:51.299
experiences and the back-end experiences
while leveraging that infrastructure.

00:19:51.299 --> 00:19:54.179
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so a question that just
came up very similar to what you

00:19:54.179 --> 00:19:55.767
were talking about, Rotica is.

00:19:55.767 --> 00:19:58.813
What are the disrupters doing
to actually streamline and

00:19:58.813 --> 00:20:00.725
innovate the regulatory process?

00:20:00.725 --> 00:20:05.961
&gt;&gt; Well, I don't know if they can
streamline the regulatory process.

00:20:05.961 --> 00:20:11.277
But the way that we've seen
the disrupters work around,

00:20:11.277 --> 00:20:19.047
or within the regulatory framework is
focusing on the front-end pieces, right?

00:20:19.047 --> 00:20:22.602
So if you think about the infrastructure
of payments and ordering.

00:20:22.602 --> 00:20:24.528
I can't help but
not think about it that way.

00:20:24.528 --> 00:20:28.953
[LAUGH] But if you think about
the infrastructure of payments, there's

00:20:28.953 --> 00:20:33.761
ways to get into that infrastructure,
and it typically goes through a bank.

00:20:33.761 --> 00:20:39.548
And so the innovators that are being
successful at it are partnering

00:20:39.548 --> 00:20:46.655
with banks to get access to that whole
world of payment's infrastructure.

00:20:46.655 --> 00:20:51.678
And looking at their services as
thinking through compliance and

00:20:51.678 --> 00:20:54.327
regulations and monitoring, and

00:20:54.327 --> 00:20:59.911
all those things makes them a very
solid stable player in the ecosystem.

00:20:59.911 --> 00:21:04.345
&gt;&gt; Yeah, one other thing I would add
that has been interesting to watch.

00:21:04.345 --> 00:21:07.955
And it's related to a few other
questions folks have in here around,

00:21:07.955 --> 00:21:09.395
kind of cost to switching.

00:21:09.395 --> 00:21:13.728
And it sounds great to move over
to some of these other solutions.

00:21:13.728 --> 00:21:18.091
But how frequently do I now have
to re-enroll and do all of that?

00:21:18.091 --> 00:21:23.029
And again, I think what we see happening
here is just leveraging digital and

00:21:23.029 --> 00:21:25.884
leveraging data that you already possess,

00:21:25.884 --> 00:21:28.907
is a far more efficient
way to do those things.

00:21:28.907 --> 00:21:31.890
So to Rotica's point on
the regulatory side.

00:21:31.890 --> 00:21:36.890
There is a specific sub-sector called reg
tech of companies that are actually trying

00:21:36.890 --> 00:21:39.930
to streamline and
automate some of the processes.

00:21:39.930 --> 00:21:42.292
And it's really about when you
think about it from a consumer and

00:21:42.292 --> 00:21:43.264
a business perspective.

00:21:43.264 --> 00:21:47.214
A lot of the data that banks,
or financial services ask for,

00:21:47.214 --> 00:21:49.081
you already have that data.

00:21:49.081 --> 00:21:50.459
But they don't make it easy for

00:21:50.459 --> 00:21:53.759
you to make that data portable to just
go ahead and give them what you need.

00:21:53.759 --> 00:21:58.238
So, everyone probably has a lot of
sore fingers from having to key so

00:21:58.238 --> 00:22:03.110
much into various online sites and
various mobile devices to provide data

00:22:03.110 --> 00:22:06.507
that actually is already
digitalized somewhere.

00:22:06.507 --> 00:22:09.328
Probably in your mobile device,
or in your digital wallet.

00:22:09.328 --> 00:22:12.135
And again, I think whether
it's in the reg tech space, or

00:22:12.135 --> 00:22:15.471
actually just some of the models
that we were talking about earlier.

00:22:15.471 --> 00:22:19.663
I think they're doing a very effective
job of minimizing the frequency in

00:22:19.663 --> 00:22:23.870
which you have to provide them data
that they can get through another way.

00:22:23.870 --> 00:22:28.830
Through your consent, or through your
permission to allow you to go ahead and

00:22:28.830 --> 00:22:29.510
provide data.

00:22:29.510 --> 00:22:34.410
And I think that, that has a huge impact
in terms of the cost of switching.

00:22:34.410 --> 00:22:38.480
I think that there's
a direct correlation that

00:22:38.480 --> 00:22:42.820
the more information you have to
provide around buying a product.

00:22:42.820 --> 00:22:47.130
Your abandonment rate, I mean there's
a lot of statistics around abandonment

00:22:47.130 --> 00:22:49.330
rate with e-commerce shopping carts.

00:22:49.330 --> 00:22:50.470
That, the more data and

00:22:50.470 --> 00:22:54.520
information you have to enter the more
frequently you're going to abandon that.

00:22:54.520 --> 00:22:58.530
I think what a lot of these
Fintech disrupters are doing

00:22:58.530 --> 00:23:01.810
are minimizing the amount of
data that you have to provide.

00:23:01.810 --> 00:23:03.990
So that you can quickly get to a buy and

00:23:03.990 --> 00:23:08.160
move your business over to their
product or their platform.

00:23:08.160 --> 00:23:11.930
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and I think that getting your,
from a switching perspective, and

00:23:11.930 --> 00:23:13.150
a management perspective.

00:23:13.150 --> 00:23:17.500
Getting your data moved over, or getting
your sort of enrollment process is one

00:23:17.500 --> 00:23:22.740
thing, and being able to manage multiple
different services that you've got.

00:23:22.740 --> 00:23:26.163
So, you're piecing together
sort of a banking solution,

00:23:26.163 --> 00:23:29.518
is something else that has
changed dramatically with some

00:23:29.518 --> 00:23:33.364
of the advances that we were talking
about in terms of technology.

00:23:33.364 --> 00:23:37.868
So before, people would want to
concentrate all of their banking and

00:23:37.868 --> 00:23:41.063
their financial services
need with one entity.

00:23:41.063 --> 00:23:45.954
Because the overhead of managing all
these different relationships and

00:23:45.954 --> 00:23:47.725
entry points was so high.

00:23:47.725 --> 00:23:50.460
Now, you've got an app for
all your services.

00:23:50.460 --> 00:23:52.219
There's notifications that come out.

00:23:52.219 --> 00:23:55.543
So there's not the need
to have everything so

00:23:55.543 --> 00:23:59.134
concentrated with one
financial institution.

00:23:59.134 --> 00:24:02.630
You can have solutions that you
manage through one interface which

00:24:02.630 --> 00:24:03.824
is your mobile phone.

00:24:03.824 --> 00:24:09.660
That's making that switch easier and
sustainable.

00:24:12.234 --> 00:24:16.154
So, I think a few questions
around how are banks

00:24:16.154 --> 00:24:20.859
responding to all of
the disruption that's happening.

00:24:20.859 --> 00:24:25.942
And I think that there's a wide variety of
things that are going on in the market.

00:24:25.942 --> 00:24:30.958
But one of the things that we see a lot
of is just some of the banks setting up

00:24:30.958 --> 00:24:36.387
their own Fintech venture capital funds
to make sure that they're looking and

00:24:36.387 --> 00:24:42.065
partnering and making investments in
companies that they can either learn from,

00:24:42.065 --> 00:24:44.570
be strategic partners with, etc.

00:24:44.570 --> 00:24:50.132
So you can see here the back
from 2/1 of 2015 to 2016 there's

00:24:50.132 --> 00:24:55.617
been a wide variety of banks that
have participated in this strategy.

00:24:55.617 --> 00:24:58.696
Either by setting up separate funds or

00:24:58.696 --> 00:25:02.747
just within their balance
sheet lending as well.

00:25:06.332 --> 00:25:13.777
And Fintech Investments, last year
was an all time high of 11.4 billion.

00:25:13.777 --> 00:25:18.566
And despite some of the easing
that's happening in funding

00:25:18.566 --> 00:25:22.493
this year across the entire
technology sector,

00:25:22.493 --> 00:25:27.008
Fintech is continuing to see
the levels of investment.

00:25:27.008 --> 00:25:31.439
And it is expected to
be even higher in 2016,

00:25:31.439 --> 00:25:36.527
primarily fueled by later
staged rounds in companies.

00:25:36.527 --> 00:25:41.088
And we'll talk about some of those
companies in a minute that are seeing some

00:25:41.088 --> 00:25:45.007
of those this bigger rounds coming
through to help sort of grow and

00:25:45.007 --> 00:25:46.949
accelerate their businesses.

00:25:46.949 --> 00:25:49.660
Is there anything else you'd add?

00:25:49.660 --> 00:25:52.650
&gt;&gt; Some of the questions that
came in were related to what you

00:25:52.650 --> 00:25:54.620
spoke on the last slide we take about.

00:25:54.620 --> 00:25:56.520
So what are the incumbents doing?

00:25:56.520 --> 00:26:00.420
They see a lot of new startups
coming into the space.

00:26:00.420 --> 00:26:05.700
They're trying to basically
offer competitive products.

00:26:05.700 --> 00:26:09.650
In addition to the investing that Rotica
spoke about a little bit earlier,

00:26:09.650 --> 00:26:14.150
I think one of the fundamental challenges
banks are going to have to deal with right

00:26:14.150 --> 00:26:19.130
now is how they think about their
brand in the market in the future.

00:26:19.130 --> 00:26:23.870
And so the reality is, Fintech has
actually existed for a long time.

00:26:23.870 --> 00:26:28.110
Because financial services buy a lot of
technology from software companies and

00:26:28.110 --> 00:26:29.490
from hardware companies.

00:26:29.490 --> 00:26:33.774
There's actually been a sector that's been
in existence for a long time of companies

00:26:33.774 --> 00:26:37.835
to specifically build products and
sell them to financial services companies.

00:26:37.835 --> 00:26:42.490
However the motto and the path is always
been we sell it to the financial services

00:26:42.490 --> 00:26:46.797
company and then they white label it or
they rebrand it with their brand and

00:26:46.797 --> 00:26:48.831
then they put it out in the market.

00:26:48.831 --> 00:26:52.836
What's been the real fundamental
shift over the past three or

00:26:52.836 --> 00:26:57.613
four years are Fintech companies that
are going directly to consumers and

00:26:57.613 --> 00:26:59.321
directly to businesses.

00:26:59.321 --> 00:27:02.518
And I think a lot of
financial services providers,

00:27:02.518 --> 00:27:07.497
going back to the last slide Rotica spoke
to, recognize that there's a need for

00:27:07.497 --> 00:27:12.277
them to properly partner differently
with some of the Fintech disruptors.

00:27:12.277 --> 00:27:17.252
But I think that this key question of
are they going to allow their brand

00:27:17.252 --> 00:27:18.187
to coexist?

00:27:18.187 --> 00:27:22.737
Or are they actually going to allow
their brand to maybe be pushed into

00:27:22.737 --> 00:27:23.992
the background and

00:27:23.992 --> 00:27:28.777
to allow one of the disruptor's brands
to be in front of the client is a key

00:27:28.777 --> 00:27:33.666
decision point that a lot of financial
institutions are grappling with.

00:27:33.666 --> 00:27:37.999
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and they're looking at it
from a demographic-by-demographic

00:27:37.999 --> 00:27:39.852
perspective as well, right?

00:27:39.852 --> 00:27:41.336
So what's that number again?

00:27:41.336 --> 00:27:46.098
70% of Millennials would rather go
to the dentist than go to the bank.

00:27:46.098 --> 00:27:50.260
So [LAUGH] there may be
a different strategy that

00:27:50.260 --> 00:27:55.140
you take with certain demographics
around putting a startup brand more

00:27:56.550 --> 00:28:00.610
in the forefront versus somebody in
a later stage of their life that is more

00:28:01.610 --> 00:28:04.020
comfortable with the bank's
brand being in front.

00:28:08.440 --> 00:28:14.403
So we were talking about a lot of money
going into the financial services or

00:28:14.403 --> 00:28:17.010
financial Fintech ecosystem.

00:28:17.010 --> 00:28:21.576
And the one thing that we want to just
talk a little bit about is that most

00:28:21.576 --> 00:28:23.953
of the value has yet to be realized.

00:28:23.953 --> 00:28:27.617
There, obviously last year,
were some exits by OnDeck, Square,

00:28:27.617 --> 00:28:32.138
LendingClub IPOing and we'll talk a little
bit about LendingClub in a little bit.

00:28:32.138 --> 00:28:35.830
I think I've seen a few
questions come through on that.

00:28:35.830 --> 00:28:41.526
But then there's this whole world of other
companies, some would call them unicorns.

00:28:41.526 --> 00:28:44.340
In the Fintech space that were the exits,

00:28:46.480 --> 00:28:49.840
there's not been exits and there's
a significant value to be realized.

00:28:53.130 --> 00:28:56.490
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so one of the questions
that just came up that I think is

00:28:56.490 --> 00:29:00.460
a good question related to everything we
talked about is kind of the demographics

00:29:00.460 --> 00:29:04.240
of Fintech clients,
whether it's consumers or businesses.

00:29:04.240 --> 00:29:07.390
And again, most of the industry
data that we look at, as

00:29:07.390 --> 00:29:10.770
much as there's a lot of hype about it's
really the Millennials and the digital

00:29:10.770 --> 00:29:14.610
natives that are driving a lot of
the adoption, actually we see it across.

00:29:14.610 --> 00:29:19.140
I think that the reality is,
I'm personally a Gen-Xer,

00:29:19.140 --> 00:29:23.420
I'm not a Millennial, but
it doesn't matter to me.

00:29:23.420 --> 00:29:27.240
A really good digital experience is
a really good digital experience.

00:29:27.240 --> 00:29:32.045
And if somebody can provide a product or
solution that is going to take 90% of

00:29:32.045 --> 00:29:37.215
the friction in the time I have to spend
enrolling or utilizing that product.

00:29:37.215 --> 00:29:38.295
It's appealing to me and

00:29:38.295 --> 00:29:43.015
I don't think that that's very different
across the different demographics.

00:29:43.015 --> 00:29:46.250
So in terms of what
the adoption rate has been and

00:29:46.250 --> 00:29:48.750
what the penetration rate has been.

00:29:48.750 --> 00:29:52.480
On the average, it is higher with
Millennials, but it's probably

00:29:52.480 --> 00:29:56.880
not as big a chasm as people would think
with respect to Gen-X or Baby Boomers.

00:29:56.880 --> 00:30:00.850
They're adopting a lot of the digital
products just as quickly as anyone.

00:30:00.850 --> 00:30:04.455
And I think probably less
around financial services.

00:30:04.455 --> 00:30:07.435
But I think the real great
use case of this is Facebook.

00:30:08.475 --> 00:30:12.055
I think probably six or seven years
ago if you asked people who would

00:30:12.055 --> 00:30:14.405
be using Facebook from
a demographic perspective,

00:30:14.405 --> 00:30:17.165
everyone would think,
well, it's young children.

00:30:17.165 --> 00:30:23.090
Actually, what you see is, it's got
tremendous adoption with the Baby Boomers.

00:30:23.090 --> 00:30:27.670
So, I don't think financial services
tech is going to be much different.

00:30:27.670 --> 00:30:31.380
I think as long as they can provide
a great digital experience and

00:30:31.380 --> 00:30:32.600
remove a lot of the friction.

00:30:33.960 --> 00:30:37.410
Probably the last comment I
would make with respect to

00:30:37.410 --> 00:30:40.600
point on most of the value has yet
to be realized.

00:30:40.600 --> 00:30:43.510
I think that we're still
in what I would call

00:30:43.510 --> 00:30:48.100
an early stage of seeing how these
companies are going to be able to scale

00:30:48.100 --> 00:30:51.790
to become very large
sustained operating models.

00:30:51.790 --> 00:30:56.550
And it's not because none
of them have done that yet,

00:30:56.550 --> 00:30:58.350
some of them have done that really well.

00:30:58.350 --> 00:31:01.740
But most of these companies have just
formed over the last five to seven years,

00:31:01.740 --> 00:31:05.110
so it's going to take some time to
see how they actually scale and

00:31:05.110 --> 00:31:08.270
how they become very large
sustainable organizations.

00:31:08.270 --> 00:31:11.630
&gt;&gt; Yeah, the other point I would make
about sort of the demographics is that

00:31:11.630 --> 00:31:14.070
several of these companies on here,
like coupa for

00:31:14.070 --> 00:31:18.810
example, is a B to B solution, right?

00:31:18.810 --> 00:31:24.650
So as the world of business
professionals are seeing the technology

00:31:24.650 --> 00:31:29.773
solutions sort of disrupting and
transforming the way that

00:31:29.773 --> 00:31:34.904
they're able to do their work or
save time and money, etc.

00:31:34.904 --> 00:31:37.738
Then it's going to trickle through
to the consumer side as well.

00:31:37.738 --> 00:31:42.326
So there is also that because it's
not just a consumer only disruption.

00:31:42.326 --> 00:31:46.877
It is on the business that there is,

00:31:46.877 --> 00:31:53.566
it has the potential to
reach the broader populous.

00:31:53.566 --> 00:31:54.468
The one thing,

00:31:54.468 --> 00:31:58.913
and I've seen a few comments up there as
well on this is that the ones that have

00:31:58.913 --> 00:32:03.320
exited sort of the OnDecks were
the Lending Clubs of the world.

00:32:03.320 --> 00:32:05.430
It's hard to ignore the fact
that they struggled

00:32:06.550 --> 00:32:09.258
a bit amongst the current
market condition.

00:32:09.258 --> 00:32:18.190
And I think that there's a variety of
reasons for some of the struggles,

00:32:18.190 --> 00:32:22.530
there's just general market
instability that's been going on.

00:32:22.530 --> 00:32:28.336
As well as just some of
the things that have happened

00:32:28.336 --> 00:32:33.478
with some of these
companies in particular.

00:32:33.478 --> 00:32:38.430
&gt;&gt; Yeah, again, I think it's very,
very, it's very early still.

00:32:38.430 --> 00:32:42.860
I think this chart will be interesting to
look at over the next three to five years.

00:32:42.860 --> 00:32:48.010
And again, I would say, if you go back and
look at other areas around innovation.

00:32:48.010 --> 00:32:51.330
You look at the early days of
when Apple became public, or

00:32:51.330 --> 00:32:54.540
even when Facebook
became public initially.

00:32:54.540 --> 00:32:57.320
Some of the challenges of
going from being a private

00:32:57.320 --> 00:32:59.130
organization to a public organization.

00:32:59.130 --> 00:33:03.050
So I think that there's not enough
data points right now to come to

00:33:03.050 --> 00:33:08.620
a conclusive position on this.

00:33:08.620 --> 00:33:13.580
But I do think that because of this,
and there's a broader macro

00:33:13.580 --> 00:33:18.080
thing going on right now,
there's just less IPOs going on right now.

00:33:18.080 --> 00:33:20.010
There's less companies
that are going public.

00:33:20.010 --> 00:33:24.490
So I think in terms of understanding
the long-term sustainability and

00:33:24.490 --> 00:33:28.170
market value proposition of
many of these business models.

00:33:28.170 --> 00:33:32.392
It's probably going to take another
five to seven years before we truly

00:33:32.392 --> 00:33:33.566
understand that.

00:33:36.214 --> 00:33:41.211
&gt;&gt; Right, and so because of this,
because of the some early innovations that

00:33:41.211 --> 00:33:44.660
are going on with
the companies that have IPOs.

00:33:44.660 --> 00:33:48.540
It is trickling down
to the private market.

00:33:48.540 --> 00:33:52.015
Given the position that we have
in the ecosystem, we have.

00:33:52.015 --> 00:33:57.196
Seeing some companies sort
of pull back a little, or

00:33:57.196 --> 00:34:02.380
have down rounds relative
to the recent fundraising

00:34:02.380 --> 00:34:07.820
that they're doing because
of the early indication.

00:34:08.920 --> 00:34:11.340
On these companies, that's Bruce's point,

00:34:11.340 --> 00:34:16.690
right, it's still early days
however we are seeing it.

00:34:16.690 --> 00:34:21.271
&gt;&gt; One of the other things I think
is unique about this sector, and

00:34:21.271 --> 00:34:25.681
I know there's been a lot of
questions about regulatory type

00:34:25.681 --> 00:34:28.379
questions around these companies.

00:34:28.379 --> 00:34:32.845
I think that first and foremost,
if you just take a step back and look at

00:34:32.845 --> 00:34:37.792
the venture backed start up community,
the reality is companies will fail.

00:34:37.792 --> 00:34:43.500
[LAUGH] That's part of the innovation
ecosystem is the reality

00:34:43.500 --> 00:34:48.341
that many start ups will not
be able to get traction.

00:34:48.341 --> 00:34:51.610
Will not be able to move
their business model forward.

00:34:51.610 --> 00:34:56.392
I think what's a little bit different in
the sector is because there's regulatory

00:34:56.392 --> 00:35:00.150
element because it involves
financial transactions, there's

00:35:00.150 --> 00:35:04.473
a different type of scrutiny around
failure in this particular industry.

00:35:04.473 --> 00:35:09.207
I've personally spent quite a bit
of time regulators over the past

00:35:09.207 --> 00:35:13.516
year talking a little bit about
how they are going to approach

00:35:13.516 --> 00:35:17.940
regulating the Fintech community
differently than banks.

00:35:17.940 --> 00:35:21.130
Because the reality is the main
goal of a regulator for

00:35:21.130 --> 00:35:26.700
a bank is to ensure they don't fail, and
the reality is within not only Fintech.

00:35:26.700 --> 00:35:30.700
But within the venture back start-up
community that there will be failures.

00:35:30.700 --> 00:35:34.470
I mean that is part of the innovation
ecosystem that happened.

00:35:34.470 --> 00:35:39.230
And in many cases actually it's
celebrated to a certain extent that fact

00:35:39.230 --> 00:35:42.280
that failure will absolutely
drive more innovation.

00:35:42.280 --> 00:35:45.850
If you're not seeing failure you're
probably not taking enough risk and

00:35:45.850 --> 00:35:47.350
you're not innovating.

00:35:47.350 --> 00:35:52.410
So I do think that's a particular area
again, the regulators are going to have to

00:35:52.410 --> 00:35:57.550
really figure out, probably a different
type of model for this sector.

00:35:57.550 --> 00:36:00.590
Because trying to just
overlay the bank model,

00:36:00.590 --> 00:36:05.790
where the bank model is heavily predicated
upon, you will actually never fail.

00:36:05.790 --> 00:36:09.090
If they overlay that model
onto to the Fintech companies,

00:36:09.090 --> 00:36:11.780
they probably will suppress
a lot on innovation.

00:36:13.210 --> 00:36:16.760
At least in the limited conversations
I've had with some regulators and

00:36:16.760 --> 00:36:17.820
some government officials.

00:36:17.820 --> 00:36:22.440
They fully recognize the need
to have a modified regulatory

00:36:22.440 --> 00:36:26.970
model around these companies
because they are not banks.

00:36:26.970 --> 00:36:31.706
And they shouldn't hold them to a, not
a similar standard, but they shouldn't try

00:36:31.706 --> 00:36:36.392
to overlay the exact same regulatory model
or probably will suppress innovation.

00:36:39.645 --> 00:36:42.472
&gt;&gt; Yeah, oops wrong button.

00:36:42.472 --> 00:36:47.298
However, given some of the challenges
that some of the disruptors are seeing in

00:36:47.298 --> 00:36:48.940
the Fintech ecosystem.

00:36:48.940 --> 00:36:54.910
Fintech will persevere and the best
companies will continue to disrupt.

00:36:54.910 --> 00:36:59.870
I think that,
with this flight is sort of going through

00:36:59.870 --> 00:37:03.120
is there's companies that
are sort of in their early days.

00:37:03.120 --> 00:37:06.227
And are sort of presenting
significantly new,

00:37:06.227 --> 00:37:11.130
unique value propositions to the model and
are starting to gain traction.

00:37:12.250 --> 00:37:16.691
The next traunch is where they've gotten
sort of significant traction in their

00:37:16.691 --> 00:37:18.100
particular markets.

00:37:18.100 --> 00:37:22.136
And then finally, with the top share or

00:37:22.136 --> 00:37:29.490
category is really interesting and
are gaining significant strides.

00:37:29.490 --> 00:37:33.843
One of the trends that we're
seeing which is really sort

00:37:33.843 --> 00:37:38.750
of prevalent amongst this top tier or
this top share category is

00:37:38.750 --> 00:37:43.210
they have entered the market
in one particular segment.

00:37:44.610 --> 00:37:50.080
Say Sofi, for example, they started
their model with lending to MBAs,

00:37:50.080 --> 00:37:54.640
students from the top 25
institutions in the country.

00:37:54.640 --> 00:37:58.620
And then have taken a broad approach to

00:37:58.620 --> 00:38:02.550
product development across
more of financial services.

00:38:02.550 --> 00:38:07.250
So the first slide we started
with banks becoming unbundled,

00:38:07.250 --> 00:38:12.450
and point solutions addressing
every single aspect of banking.

00:38:12.450 --> 00:38:16.190
Now, we're seeing disruptors that started
with that approach of let me unbundle,

00:38:16.190 --> 00:38:20.640
let me do that one thing better,
faster, more seamlessly.

00:38:20.640 --> 00:38:24.920
And there's coming back and
rebundling some things together

00:38:24.920 --> 00:38:29.300
in order to have a more holistic
solution to offer to the market.

00:38:29.300 --> 00:38:34.550
So we see that pretty much in all of these
companies, that they are going back and

00:38:34.550 --> 00:38:39.590
trying to rebundle more things together
in order to continue to drive the market.

00:38:39.590 --> 00:38:42.180
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
I would say the other point to that

00:38:42.180 --> 00:38:44.810
the most of these companies
are doing it again.

00:38:44.810 --> 00:38:48.060
It's very consistent with
what you would see across

00:38:48.060 --> 00:38:49.960
the innovation start up community.

00:38:49.960 --> 00:38:53.660
And that is they try to get some
product in market as fast as

00:38:53.660 --> 00:38:56.120
possible to ideate and learn.

00:38:56.120 --> 00:39:01.160
And again, I think that where one of
the challenges the incumbents have,

00:39:01.160 --> 00:39:05.280
is if you think about the model
today around product design,

00:39:05.280 --> 00:39:08.675
product development,
introducing something into the market.

00:39:08.675 --> 00:39:13.320
It's a very long cycle for a traditional
financial institution to do that.

00:39:13.320 --> 00:39:16.727
A lot of them are thinking
about accelerating that cycle,

00:39:16.727 --> 00:39:18.683
including in our organization.

00:39:18.683 --> 00:39:23.462
But I think the model of developing,
finding a pain point, like Ratika said, or

00:39:23.462 --> 00:39:25.219
finding a point of friction,

00:39:25.219 --> 00:39:30.021
quickly getting a product into the market
and then doing rapid ideation on that.

00:39:30.021 --> 00:39:33.344
And then doing horizontal
product expansion off of

00:39:33.344 --> 00:39:38.287
that gives them an opportunity to quickly
respond to changes in the market,

00:39:38.287 --> 00:39:41.080
quickly respond to client expectations.

00:39:41.080 --> 00:39:43.609
And again, most of these companies on

00:39:43.609 --> 00:39:48.524
here will be releasing product on
a weekly basis, if not on a daily basis.

00:39:48.524 --> 00:39:53.386
Which is very unusual for a financial
services organization to do releases

00:39:53.386 --> 00:39:56.670
that quickly of enhancements and
new features.

00:39:56.670 --> 00:40:00.150
And I think that's a clear
point of differentiation and

00:40:00.150 --> 00:40:02.000
advantage that they have
in the market right now.

00:40:04.806 --> 00:40:09.524
&gt;&gt; So I think that's it in terms
of what we've got from a prepared

00:40:09.524 --> 00:40:14.896
material perspective but I know
there's a bunch of questions [LAUGH].

00:40:14.896 --> 00:40:17.090
&gt;&gt; A pretty intriguing question is,

00:40:17.090 --> 00:40:20.578
will there be a point where
banks are no longer needed?

00:40:20.578 --> 00:40:23.782
And it's something that we've
spent a lot time talking about,

00:40:23.782 --> 00:40:26.810
less around whether or
not banks will be no longer needed.

00:40:26.810 --> 00:40:29.910
But what could potentially
be the future role of

00:40:29.910 --> 00:40:32.000
traditional financial institutions.

00:40:32.000 --> 00:40:36.670
I'll start and
then I'll let Reetika weigh in here.

00:40:36.670 --> 00:40:41.742
So how we think that this potentially is
going to evolve is, so first and foremost,

00:40:41.742 --> 00:40:47.310
financial institutions have a lot of
incredible assets and infrastructure.

00:40:47.310 --> 00:40:52.040
They have trillions of dollars
of federally insured deposits,

00:40:52.040 --> 00:40:55.965
which are basically the oil for
the engine.

00:40:55.965 --> 00:40:58.322
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; To do lending and to do payments, and

00:40:58.322 --> 00:41:00.220
to provide liquidity services.

00:41:00.220 --> 00:41:04.520
So they have an incredible asset with
respect to their balance sheets,

00:41:04.520 --> 00:41:05.850
their infrastructure.

00:41:05.850 --> 00:41:11.090
The fact that they are regulated, they
are trusted, they have access to a lot

00:41:11.090 --> 00:41:15.990
of the payment clearing and settlement
systems, which are incredibly valuable.

00:41:15.990 --> 00:41:19.850
So what we think about is kind
of the distinction between

00:41:19.850 --> 00:41:24.110
all of the great infrastructure
services that a bank can provide and

00:41:24.110 --> 00:41:26.790
then kind of the consumer facing products.

00:41:26.790 --> 00:41:31.700
And, not the best analogy, but
one of the things we think

00:41:31.700 --> 00:41:36.010
about all the time is kind of how
Apple has evolved their model.

00:41:36.010 --> 00:41:40.580
And it's really interesting, and Apple,
either the largest or second largest,

00:41:40.580 --> 00:41:44.720
I haven't looked today on their
market cap versus Google's, but

00:41:44.720 --> 00:41:50.180
in terms of what they provide in
terms of an operating platform.

00:41:50.180 --> 00:41:54.420
And I think people, they think about
their Apple device but the reality is,

00:41:54.420 --> 00:41:58.720
they're using so many other products
that are built off of the hardware and

00:41:58.720 --> 00:42:01.160
the operating system,
off of the Apple device.

00:42:01.160 --> 00:42:05.770
And so I think that what we could
see evolving with some financial

00:42:05.770 --> 00:42:11.960
institutions is them being maybe more
of an operating platform provider.

00:42:11.960 --> 00:42:17.300
Allowing a lot of other software or
client facing technologies that,

00:42:17.300 --> 00:42:22.210
again, can leverage their infrastructure,
can leverage their assets in

00:42:22.210 --> 00:42:27.940
a way that they can still monetize that,
provide some very valuable services.

00:42:27.940 --> 00:42:31.407
But get the network effect, and I'm
stealing a little of Reetika's thunder,

00:42:31.407 --> 00:42:34.476
because the network effect is something
she talks about all the time.

00:42:34.476 --> 00:42:38.685
Of actually having other companies that
are out there originating business for

00:42:38.685 --> 00:42:41.650
you off of your platform or
off of your infrastructure.

00:42:41.650 --> 00:42:46.920
So we see kind of, possibly not for every
single traditional financial institution,

00:42:46.920 --> 00:42:50.040
but for some kind of an evolution
to a model like that.

00:42:50.040 --> 00:42:54.840
But in terms of the broader question,
will banks need to be in existence?

00:42:54.840 --> 00:43:00.135
Some form of an entity that can manage
federally insured deposits on behalf

00:43:00.135 --> 00:43:05.348
of consumers and can provide some of
the major infrastructure services,

00:43:05.348 --> 00:43:07.464
we think will be a necessity.

00:43:07.464 --> 00:43:08.547
&gt;&gt; Yeah,no, I mean,

00:43:08.547 --> 00:43:12.450
I think that you've pretty much summarized
what I was going to say as well.

00:43:12.450 --> 00:43:16.700
But I did kind of allude to it
earlier is that companies today,

00:43:16.700 --> 00:43:20.628
the startups today that
are successfully innovating and

00:43:20.628 --> 00:43:26.382
successfully disrupting in the backend
are actually partnering with many banks.

00:43:26.382 --> 00:43:30.171
And we are, that we'll work with
a start up and help them gain access

00:43:30.171 --> 00:43:34.761
to the infrastructure that helps power the
ecosystem while they spend the time and

00:43:34.761 --> 00:43:37.506
energy focusing on
the front-end experience.

00:43:37.506 --> 00:43:40.638
And gaining that network
effect of reaching

00:43:40.638 --> 00:43:44.708
a broad customer base with
their particular solutions.

00:43:44.708 --> 00:43:48.615
So, yeah I'm very much in
agreement that I think that that

00:43:48.615 --> 00:43:52.942
infrastructure is still going to
be very important in the future.

00:43:52.942 --> 00:43:55.778
&gt;&gt; And there have been a lot
of questions about whether or

00:43:55.778 --> 00:43:59.950
not this has been recorded and whether or
not the materials will be available.

00:43:59.950 --> 00:44:01.450
And yes they will be available.

00:44:01.450 --> 00:44:01.950
Correct?

00:44:05.032 --> 00:44:07.290
Okay, the recording will be available.

00:44:07.290 --> 00:44:09.160
The slides will not be available.

00:44:12.460 --> 00:44:15.851
So another question that just came in,

00:44:15.851 --> 00:44:21.106
which is pretty interesting,
is trying to summarize this.

00:44:21.106 --> 00:44:22.374
This is kind of a long question.

00:44:22.374 --> 00:44:29.545
But in terms of what we have found
through our own experiences is the most

00:44:29.545 --> 00:44:34.817
valuable ways to improve
the user experience or UI.

00:44:34.817 --> 00:44:36.672
Since a lot of this comes
down to the experience.

00:44:36.672 --> 00:44:40.538
Well, I'll let you start with that one,
Reetika, just in terms of what we've seen

00:44:40.538 --> 00:44:44.295
in terms of working within tech companies
and venture backed companies on how they

00:44:44.295 --> 00:44:48.145
approach that maybe a little bit
differently than the incumbents.

00:44:48.145 --> 00:44:51.970
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean,
I think that one thing that we see, and

00:44:51.970 --> 00:44:57.750
I mentioned that I run the accelerator
program in conjunction with MasterCard,

00:44:57.750 --> 00:44:59.882
called Commerce Innovated.

00:44:59.882 --> 00:45:04.869
So we some very, very early stage
companies that come with their ideas,

00:45:04.869 --> 00:45:08.240
and their businesses and
how to bring to market.

00:45:08.240 --> 00:45:13.340
And one thing that we see predominantly
among those companies is, they're

00:45:13.340 --> 00:45:17.775
all trying to solve a particular pain
point that they've experienced themselves,

00:45:17.775 --> 00:45:21.600
from frustration that
they've got with the UI or

00:45:21.600 --> 00:45:25.720
UX that they've been struggling with.

00:45:25.720 --> 00:45:28.362
So they bring that sort of
personal experience and

00:45:28.362 --> 00:45:33.770
that personal perspective to
the solutions that they bring to market.

00:45:33.770 --> 00:45:38.101
The other thing is,
they're taking influences from

00:45:38.101 --> 00:45:42.624
technology companies instead of banks,
so it's the,

00:45:42.624 --> 00:45:46.859
how do I vote things up and
down in Facebook versus how

00:45:46.859 --> 00:45:51.594
do I express preferences in
the world of banking, right?

00:45:51.594 --> 00:45:57.558
So, they're taking cues from the ecosystem
of technology providers that they like and

00:45:57.558 --> 00:46:02.000
pulling those together and
sort of driving unique experiences.

00:46:03.360 --> 00:46:07.335
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and I would just say the last
thing is a little redundant from what I

00:46:07.335 --> 00:46:11.584
said earlier is, they start with
customer or business expectations first.

00:46:11.584 --> 00:46:17.090
And really, kind of the design process
starts with the experience with

00:46:17.090 --> 00:46:24.200
the customer with the business, as opposed
to the design starting with a product.

00:46:24.200 --> 00:46:28.720
And that's kind of a profound difference
in user experience development is really

00:46:28.720 --> 00:46:32.560
starting with the overall end-to-end
experience that you're trying to deliver.

00:46:32.560 --> 00:46:36.190
And to Reetika's point, most of the times
it's around solving a pain point or

00:46:36.190 --> 00:46:38.120
eliminating friction from the process.

00:46:38.120 --> 00:46:42.580
And then the product is
kind of built from there.

00:46:42.580 --> 00:46:45.426
The other thing that I think
that they do very well.

00:46:45.426 --> 00:46:49.602
The ones that do this very well have
a recognition of the necessity for

00:46:49.602 --> 00:46:51.940
backwards compatibility.

00:46:51.940 --> 00:46:55.040
And what I mean by that is,
a lot of these things,

00:46:55.040 --> 00:46:59.630
a lot of these models are built
on other infrastructure.

00:46:59.630 --> 00:47:03.510
Or they have to have access to
existing clearing networks or

00:47:03.510 --> 00:47:05.020
existing payment networks.

00:47:05.020 --> 00:47:09.460
And creating that level of backwards
compatibility is very important.

00:47:09.460 --> 00:47:15.530
So again, I think they start with trying
to design a user experience first and

00:47:15.530 --> 00:47:19.940
then insuring that there's backwards
compatibility about how that then is

00:47:19.940 --> 00:47:23.970
engineered and
configured to work within the existing.

00:47:23.970 --> 00:47:28.897
So again, many times it's just about
building a complete abstraction layer

00:47:28.897 --> 00:47:30.489
between the consumer and

00:47:30.489 --> 00:47:34.760
all of the kind of friction that
can be created around the product.

00:47:34.760 --> 00:47:38.629
Another interesting question
that came up was around PayPal,

00:47:38.629 --> 00:47:41.994
in terms of their role around
the Fintech revolution.

00:47:41.994 --> 00:47:45.348
And I would say that they're
one of the pioneers [LAUGH].

00:47:45.348 --> 00:47:50.438
So I think PayPal is unquestionably
one of the first fintech companies

00:47:50.438 --> 00:47:57.000
that did have a consumer direct product
and a small business direct product.

00:47:57.000 --> 00:47:58.350
And again, if you go back, and

00:47:58.350 --> 00:48:02.970
it's been awhile, but they were
solving something very, very specific.

00:48:02.970 --> 00:48:06.819
What was very challenging, and that was,
how could individuals exchange value

00:48:06.819 --> 00:48:09.943
between each other if they weren't
physically with each other?

00:48:09.943 --> 00:48:13.284
[LAUGH] And as much as we take that for
granted today,

00:48:13.284 --> 00:48:16.560
the ease in which,
that didn't exist back then.

00:48:16.560 --> 00:48:20.345
So the idea of,
if I was in one physical location and

00:48:20.345 --> 00:48:22.610
Reetika was in another physical location,
and

00:48:22.610 --> 00:48:27.875
we wanted to conduct commerce,
it was really difficult to do that.

00:48:27.875 --> 00:48:32.850
And again combined with kind of
the birth of eBay and the idea that

00:48:32.850 --> 00:48:36.890
there could be a digital marketplace that
people could go to to conduct commerce.

00:48:36.890 --> 00:48:41.160
I think that absolutely was a key
aspect of helping PayPal get launched.

00:48:41.160 --> 00:48:47.440
But they solved something that was really
big and that was how to create a digital

00:48:47.440 --> 00:48:51.990
experience where stored value could be
exchanged between two individuals, that

00:48:51.990 --> 00:48:56.000
not only are not physically in the same
place, but don't even know each other.

00:48:56.000 --> 00:49:00.837
They created a trusted network where
they could between two individuals who

00:49:00.837 --> 00:49:05.297
wanted to conduct commerce and
really kind of facilitated a way to do it

00:49:05.297 --> 00:49:08.639
digitally that really
didn't exist prior to that.

00:49:08.639 --> 00:49:14.135
So, they were really incredible pioneers,
and they're obviously an unbelievable

00:49:14.135 --> 00:49:19.018
success story just in terms of, going
back to what we talked about earlier,

00:49:19.018 --> 00:49:23.890
about can these companies build
sustainable, growing organizations.

00:49:23.890 --> 00:49:26.325
I think PayPal has absolutely
proven to do that.

00:49:26.325 --> 00:49:26.825
&gt;&gt; Yep.

00:49:29.198 --> 00:49:31.570
&gt;&gt; One or two other questions?

00:49:31.570 --> 00:49:36.426
&gt;&gt; There's a question in here about,
stage do you recommend a fintech

00:49:36.426 --> 00:49:40.711
startup to approach a venture
capital of EC to raise money?

00:49:40.711 --> 00:49:45.690
And there's a few different
answers to that question, and

00:49:45.690 --> 00:49:48.230
I'm sure Bruce has got
some opinions as well.

00:49:48.230 --> 00:49:53.396
But I think that today,
venture capitalists tend to look for

00:49:53.396 --> 00:49:58.967
specific factors when they
are investing in a brand new company,

00:49:58.967 --> 00:50:01.920
early, early, earliest stage.

00:50:01.920 --> 00:50:06.769
And I think today there are so many
different ways to raise capital outside of

00:50:06.769 --> 00:50:11.619
the traditional VC model based on
the level of experience that you've got,

00:50:11.619 --> 00:50:13.589
bringing new ideas to market,

00:50:13.589 --> 00:50:18.550
some of those alternative methods might
be a good way to get started, right.

00:50:18.550 --> 00:50:21.785
There's angel investing,
there's crowd funding.

00:50:21.785 --> 00:50:26.210
We talked a little bit,
Kickstarter was on one of our slides.

00:50:26.210 --> 00:50:28.054
There's AngelList, etc.

00:50:28.054 --> 00:50:32.880
So, there may be a point where some
of those alternative methods of

00:50:32.880 --> 00:50:37.900
getting some seed funding in place
might be a better strategy than trying

00:50:37.900 --> 00:50:43.350
to go straight to a VC firm to raise
money to get your product off the ground.

00:50:43.350 --> 00:50:46.920
And then bringing in somebody
of a more strategic nature,

00:50:46.920 --> 00:50:51.850
like a venture capitalist, to help you
when you're at an inflection point is

00:50:51.850 --> 00:50:55.960
more of what we're seeing in the market.

00:50:55.960 --> 00:51:01.240
For bringing new founders,
for repeat entrepreneurs,

00:51:01.240 --> 00:51:05.640
going directly to the VC
is still happening.

00:51:05.640 --> 00:51:09.499
&gt;&gt; Yeah, the last comment I would make
since we are at the top of the hour is,

00:51:09.499 --> 00:51:13.129
I think for fintech companies in
terms of getting venture funding.

00:51:13.129 --> 00:51:18.011
Two things that they probably have to
do that's a little different than other

00:51:18.011 --> 00:51:22.366
venture backed tech companies is one is,
I think they have to show and

00:51:22.366 --> 00:51:27.650
demonstrate that they've got a robust
compliance risk management and security.

00:51:27.650 --> 00:51:30.290
And I would understrike security,

00:51:30.290 --> 00:51:35.220
because again you're now dealing with
some element of financial transactions.

00:51:35.220 --> 00:51:38.200
And I know there were a few questions
earlier about cyber security and

00:51:38.200 --> 00:51:42.290
I think cyber security is important
in almost any industry, but

00:51:42.290 --> 00:51:44.830
in this particular industry
it is at the absolute top.

00:51:44.830 --> 00:51:49.350
And the last comment I would make is, I
think that you really need to demonstrate

00:51:49.350 --> 00:51:54.160
that you have a client acquisition and
more importantly a client retention model.

00:51:54.160 --> 00:51:58.150
One of the things around fintech that
again is slightly different than some

00:51:58.150 --> 00:52:02.790
of other tech models,
is it can't be a transactional model.

00:52:02.790 --> 00:52:06.090
It has to be a relationship model
where you're going to get sustained

00:52:06.090 --> 00:52:09.940
continuous transactions from
the individual on your platform.

00:52:09.940 --> 00:52:13.880
And showing that you can not do just
a single transaction with a consumer,

00:52:13.880 --> 00:52:18.030
but you can actually have
a model that you can continue to

00:52:18.030 --> 00:52:20.830
sell to that consumer is very important.

00:52:20.830 --> 00:52:22.490
I think we're at the top of the hour.

00:52:22.490 --> 00:52:25.340
&gt;&gt; I think so.
So again thank you Reetika and Bruce.

00:52:25.340 --> 00:52:29.420
Very informative and enlightening and
thank you all for attending.

00:52:29.420 --> 00:52:33.510
I hope you will come back again soon and
attend one of our other webinars.

00:52:33.510 --> 00:52:36.366
So have a great day, wherever you
might be in the world at this point.

